export const LAGOS_STORY: StorySection[] = [
  { title:"1980s – The Okrika Era", body:"Before the internet. Before StockX. Before Instagram. In Lagos, the first sneakers weren't bought — they were inherited. Pairs of Reeboks and Nikes arrived through relatives returning from London, New York, or Houston. They came folded inside suitcases with 'jand things.' Children fought over who got to wear them to church on Sunday. Meanwhile, the Aba markets produced their own: local interpretations of silhouettes nobody could afford. Okrika — second-hand imports from America and Europe — was how Lagos first tasted global sneaker culture. You could find a Nike Air Max in a bundle of clothes at Yaba market for 200 naira in 1994." },
  { title:"1990s – MTV Base and the NBA Pipeline", body:"Channel O launched in 1995. MTV Base followed. Lagos teenagers were suddenly watching Allen Iverson in Reeboks, Michael Jordan in Jordan Brand, Will Smith in Air Force 1s. The NBA was a Lagos religion before anyone in the West understood this. Kanu Nwankwo and Jay-Jay Okocha were the bridge — professional Nigerians in Europe who came home wearing what nobody here could buy. The okrika markets started getting sorted. Traders who knew what an Air Jordan was could charge ten times the price. Knowledge was currency." },
  { title:"2000s – Naija Music Builds the Foundation", body:"D'Banj dropped Tongolo in 2005 wearing a fitted cap and Jordans. 2Face Idibia performed in Air Force 1s. P-Square music videos looked like American rap videos — intentionally. The Lagos music industry was building a mirror to American hip-hop culture and sneakers were the costume of choice. Alaba International Market started stocking Grade-A replicas. Oshodi traders knew Nike SKU numbers. Sneaker culture was here — it was just running underground." },
  { title:"2010s – Instagram Changes the Game", body:"Instagram launched in 2010. By 2013, Lagos sneakerheads had found each other. The hashtag #LagosSneakers appeared. WhatsApp groups called things like 'Sneaker Plug Lagos' and 'Heat Check 9ja' had 250 members sharing cop or drop advice on Jordan 4 Military Blues and Yeezy 750s. The mainland vs. island divide became sneaker geography: Lekki-Victoria Island wore anything that cost above ₦100K. Yaba, Surulere, and Agege wore heat that clocked knowledge, not price. Different currencies, same culture." },
  { title:"Afrobeats Becomes the Loudspeaker", body:"When Davido posted a photo in Off-White x Nike Dunks in 2018, Lagos sneaker twitter broke. Wizkid photographed in Jordan 1s on British Vogue covers. Burna Boy wore a different pair of Jordans to every interview during the African Giant era. These weren't endorsements. These were personal collections. Nigerian musicians, finally earning globally, were spending globally — and Lagos youth were watching every pair closely. The Afrobeats-sneaker pipeline is the most powerful cultural driver of sneaker demand in West Africa." },
  { title:"Victor Osimhen and the Football Bridge", body:"Osimhen won the Serie A Golden Boot in 2023. His sneaker posts on Instagram pulled hundreds of thousands of Lagos viewers into collections that included Travis Scott Jordans, Yeezy 700s, and Off-White Nike collabs. Nigerian football culture has always been aspirational. But post-Osimhen, the conversation changed. Lagos youth didn't just want to watch the Super Eagles — they wanted to dress like the stars who played for them. Football became the third pipeline into sneaker culture alongside music and fashion." },
  { title:"The Economics of Sneakers in Lagos", body:"The Lagos sneaker market runs on three economies. First: the authorised grey market — premium stores stocking retail-priced pairs at dollar-conversion rates. Second: the import economy — Lagos traders flying to Dubai, London, or New York to buy fresh releases and selling them at 20-40% markup. Third: the resell economy — Instagram and Jiji listings of deadstock pairs at whatever the market will bear. The Naira depreciation crisis of 2022-2024 hit the sneaker market hard. A ₦170,000 Jordan 1 became ₦280,000 almost overnight. The market adapted. Serious collectors focused on value-hold investments: limited pairs that appreciate." },
  { title:"Sneakers Fest 2026 – The Exhibition Mission", body:"Sneakers Fest 2026 isn't just an event. It's a cultural stake in the ground. A declaration that Lagos has its own sneaker history — its own relationship to these objects. The exhibition isn't about copying Sneaker Con or ComplexCon. It's about telling the Lagos version of this story: the okrika markets, the Alaba replicas, the WhatsApp groups, the Afrobeats ambassadors, the football pipeline, the mainland grails. This is where global sneaker culture meets Lagos identity. December 12, 2026. Muri Okunola Park. Lagos declares itself." },
]

